WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.420
Film Courage: Top 3 movies every actor should
watch and which performance they should study

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.220
(that is, in terms of the acting performance)?

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:12.880
Doesn’t mean the story but whether it’s
a film like GLENGARRY GLEN ROSS, whether it’s

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.220
NETWORK, just in terms of acting and performances.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.220
Mark W. Travis: Okay.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.630
Top movies and top performances are two different
categories.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:23.830
Film Courage: Okay.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:24.830
Top performances.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:25.830
Let me narrow it down.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.910
Mark W. Travis: Top performances, BLUE JASMINE.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.170
Film Courage: Oh, yes!

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:30.750
Mark W. Travis: Cate Blanchett.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:35.710
To me it was a stunning performance because
in terms of the way I work, I think there

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:40.390
are certain actors, when I say actors can’t
do this alone, there are some actors who have

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:47.170
these amazing abilities to allow themselves
to sink deep into a character.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:52.789
Cate Blanchett is one of them and BLUE JASMINE’s
one of those performances almost regardless

00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:53.989
of what you think of the movie.

00:00:53.989 --> 00:01:00.740
You just watch her work and the multi-faceted
ways because of different timeframes she had

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.840
to experience that character.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:11.149
Another actor Daniel Day-Lewis if you look
at his work in LINCOLN or GANGS OF NEW YORK

00:01:11.149 --> 00:01:12.149
or something.

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:20.450
He’s another actor who has found a way of
dropping himself deeply into a character and

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:26.139
(very quickly) one interview I read about
him (not with him), an actress he worked with

00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:29.179
him asked him at the end of the movie
(because she couldn’t talk to him during

00:01:29.179 --> 00:01:33.789
the movie because he was in character all
the time), asked him why he spent so much time

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:37.759
and so much energy just to get into a character
like that.

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:42.639
And his answer was…he says “I’m not
a good enough actor to do it any other way.”

00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:44.389
Which I thought was profound.

00:01:44.389 --> 00:01:52.159
He sees himself as a very limited actor but
he knows what he needs to do to get deep into

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:53.439
that character.

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:58.309
If you watch MANCHESTER BY THE SEA, Casey
Affleck’s performance I think is stunning

00:01:58.309 --> 00:02:03.369
regardless of his history, his background
and the problems that he has, watch that performance

00:02:03.369 --> 00:02:08.170
because there’s another actor who has dropped
so deeply into the nuances of the characters,

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.739
it’s sort of startling.

00:02:10.739 --> 00:02:13.360
Meryl Streep will do this frequently…Frequently?

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.909
She’s only been nominated 20 times.

00:02:16.909 --> 00:02:24.880
But there are certain actors who you watch
them and they can literally disappear, you

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.599
feel that the character takes on and that’s
what we are looking for.

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:30.709
Film Courage: Chameleons?

00:02:30.709 --> 00:02:36.319
Mark W. Travis: Chameleons in a way but I
see it as…more than a chameleon.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:41.599
Chameleon is more like I will change myself
into something which it is chameleon like

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:49.330
but it is I will release myself, I will give
myself over (this is my experience of these

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.269
actors) is that they give themselves over
and just allow themselves to be in that world

00:02:54.269 --> 00:03:02.370
with…in a way…ignoring themselves regardless
of what happens to them as an individual,

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.370
as an actor.

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.829
They have to be that, that’s a total relinquishment
to the character.

00:03:07.829 --> 00:03:12.760
Film Courage: And if someone were to watch
let’s say those performances from those

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:17.560
four or five actors that you mentioned, what
about turning down the sound and just seeing

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.629
the body language, the reactions, the expressions,
the micro-expressions.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:24.159
Mark W. Travis: Yeah.

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:30.900
Watch them exactly, watch them in detail and
watch the movies (which Elsha and I do.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:36.680
We can have a whole discussion about that),
we watch it several times and watch and even

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:42.870
stop and look at and go back and just look
at that moment, look at that reaction and

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:49.760
understand that the best performances, that
reaction was not planned.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.829
That stunning reaction probably was not planned
at all, it just happened.

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:59.250
I remember I was working with an editor that
I had worked with on a couple films of mine

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.790
(he was showing me a film that I didn’t
direct).

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:05.530
There was a scene and a stunning moment in
that scene he says “You know that’s the

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.410
only time she ever did that?”

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:13.751
And that to me was a key thing, to capture
those moments that happen but first of all

00:04:13.751 --> 00:04:17.449
the actor has to allow themselves to give
that unplanned.

00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:21.109
Planned performances are death!

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:23.470
Unplanned performances are where the gold
is.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.850
Just go in there and just see what happens.

